{{smaller block|The ''throstel'' old, the frosty feldefare.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Parliament of Fowls, l. 364.}}
I herde the jaye, and the ''throstelle'',
The mawys menyde of hir songe.
{{right|''Thomas of Ersseldoune'' (Child's Ballads, I. 98).}}
The ''throstle'' with his note so true,
The wren with little quill.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. N. D., iii. 1. 130.}}
In the gloamin o' the wood
The ''throssil'' whusslit sweet.
{{right|''Motherwell'', Jeanie Morrison.}}}}

2. A machine for spinning wool, cotton, etc., from the rove, consisting of a set of drawing-rollers with bobbins and fliers, and differing from the mule in having the twisting-apparatus stationary, and also in that it twists and winds simultaneously and continuously. {{smaller|Yarn from the throstle is smooth, and is used for sewing-thread and the warp of heavy goods, while yarn from the mule is soft and downy, and is used for the weft of heavy goods, and both warp and weft of light goods. Also called ''water-frame'', because at first driven by water, and originating in the water-frame of Arkwright. See cut under ''water-frame''. Also ''throstle-frame''.}}

