¶
{{smaller block|Yarn, as delivered from the mule in woollen-spinning, or from the ''throstle'' in the case of worsteds, is in the condition known as singles.
{{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', XXIV. 659.}}}}

'''throstle-cock''' (thros′l-kok), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. also ''thrustle-cock'', ''thresel-cock''; < ME. ''throstelcok'', ''throstelkok'', ''throstylkock'', ''thrustelcok'', ''threstelcok'', ''thyrstyllecok''; < ''throstle'' + ''cock''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. ''thricecock''.] The male mistlethrush. [Prov. Eng.]

{{smaller block|The ousel and the ''throstle-cocke'',
Chief musick of our Maye.
{{right|''Drayton'', Shepherd's Garland. (''Nares''.)}}
Methinks I hear the ''thresel-cock'',
Methinks I hear the jaye.
{{right|''Little Musgrave and Lady Barnard'' (Child's Ballads, II. 18).}}}}

'''throstle-frame''' (thros′l-frām), ''n.'' Same as ''throstle'', 2.

'''throstling''' (thros′ling), ''n.'' [Appar. < ''throstle'' + ''-ing''<sup>1</sup>, after ''thrush''<sup>2</sup> confused with ''thrush''<sup>1</sup> (?).] A disease of cattle occasioned by a swelling under the throat.

'''throttle''' (throt′l), ''n.'' [< ME. *''throtel'' = G. ''drossel'', the throat; dim. of ''throat''.] 1. The throat. {{smaller|(''a'') The gullet or swallow: same as ''throat'', 2 (''a'').}}

{{smaller block|Leaving all claretless the unmoistened ''throttle''.
{{right|''Byron'', Don Juan, xiv. 58.}}}}

{{smaller|(''b'') The windpipe or thropple: same as ''throat'', 2 (''b'').}}

{{smaller block|Æneas with that vision stricken down,
Well nere bestraught, vpstart his heare for dread,
Amid his ''throatel'' his voice likewise 'gan stick.
{{right|''Surrey'', Æneid, iv. 361.}}
At the upper extream it [the bittern] hath no fit larinx or ''throttle'' to quallifie the sound, and at the other end by two branches deriveth itself into the lungs.
{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Vulg. Err., iii. 27.}}}}

2. A throttle-valve.

{{smaller block|If the engine is not fitted with driver-brakes, he must}}

readings.c1s3:
reverse the engine and again open the ''throttle''.

{{right|''Scribner's Mag.'', VI. 332.}}

'''throttle''' (throt′l), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''throttled'', ppr. ''throttling''. [< ME. ''throtlen'' (= G. ''er-drosseln''); < ''throttle'', ''n''.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To choke; suffocate; have the throat obstructed so as to be in danger of suffocation. ''Imp. Dict.''—'''2.''' To breathe hard, as when nearly suffocated. ''Imp. Dict.''

'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To choke; suffocate; stop the breath of by compressing the throat; strangle.

{{smaller block|'Tis but to pull the pillow from his head,<br>And he is ''throttled''. {{right|''B. Jonson'', Volpone, ii. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|They seized him, pulled him down, and would probably soon have ''throttled'' him. {{right|''Scott'', Quentin Durward, xxxiii.}}}}

'''2'''†. To pronounce with a choking voice; utter with breaks and interruptions, like a person half suffocated.

{{smaller block|I have seen them shiver and look pale,<br>Make periods in the midst of sentences,<br>''Throttle'' their practised accent in their fears. {{right|''Shak.'', M. N. D., v. 1. 97.}}}}

'''3.''' To obstruct by a throttle-valve or otherwise: said of steam, a steam-pipe, or a steam-engine.

{{smaller block|When the ports and passages offer much resistance, the steam is expressively said to be ''throttled'' or wire-drawn. {{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', XXII. 487.}}}}

{{smaller block|The engine was running nearly at full power, very slightly ''throttled''. {{right|''The Engineer'', LXV. 430.}}}}

='''Syn.''' '''1.''' ''Strangle'', etc. See ''smother''.

readings.c2s2:
to creep ''through'' a hole; to march ''through'' the streets; to see ''through'' a telescope; to cut ''through'' several thicknesses; to pass ''through'' a doorway. Sometimes emphatically reduplicated, as in the phrase ''through and through.''

{{smaller block|Thy slander hath gone ''through and through'' her heart.
{{right|''Shak.'', Much Ado, v. 1. 68.}}}}

{{smaller block|I'd make this ten mile forty mile about,
Before I'd ride ''through'' any market-town.
{{right|''Middleton (and others)'', The Widow, iii. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|Oftentimes they vse for swords the horne of a Deere put ''through'' a peece of wood in forme of a Pickaxe.
{{right|''Capt. John Smith'', Works, I. 132.}}}}

{{smaller block|The Court could not see . . . that the nation had outgrown its old institutions, . . . was pressing against them, and would soon burst ''through'' them.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Lord Bacon.}}}}

{{smaller block|If we look ''through'' a pane of red glass, rays which come ''through'' it to the eye from a white object will be red.
{{right|''Amer. Jour. Psychol.'', II. 638.}}}}

(''b'') From the beginning to the end of; in or during the course of; coincident with: as, to enjoy good health all ''through'' life.

{{smaller block|They alledge the antiquity of Episcopacy ''through'' all Ages.
{{right|''Milton'', Reformation in Eng., ii.}}}}

{{smaller block|A shapeless mound, cumbrous with its very strength, and overgrown, ''through'' long years of peace and neglect, with grass and alien weeds.
{{right|''Hawthorne'', Scarlet Letter, Int., p. 22.}}}}

(''c'') Throughout; over the whole surface or extent of; in all directions in; all over: as, to travel ''through'' the country.

{{smaller block|In the same Prouince of Tanguth is Succuir, whose Mountaines are clothed with Rheubarbe, from whence it is by Merchants conueyed ''through'' the World.
{{right|''Purchas'', Pilgrimage, p. 427.}}}}

{{smaller block|By us, your Fame shall ''thro''' the World be blaz'd.
{{right|''Congreve'', tr. of Ovid's Art of Love.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|Mental emotions undoubtedly destroy life by the overwhelming perturbation which they produce ''through'' the whole nervous system.
{{right|''J. M. Carnochan'', Operative Surgery, p. 98.}}}}

{{smaller block|(''d'') Expressing passage in and out of, among, along, or within some yielding medium, or separable or penetrable aggregate: as, to move ''through'' the water, as a fish or a ship; to wander ''through'' the jungle; to run the fingers ''through'' the hair.}}

{{smaller block|Afore I will endure such another half day with him, I'll be drawn with a good gib-cat ''through'' the great pond at home.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Bartholomew Fair, i. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|We glide serenely enough ''through'' still deep reaches where the current is insignificant.
{{right|''Fortnightly Rev.'', N. S., XLIII. 629.}}}}

{{smaller block|(''e'') Expressing complete passage from one step to another in any series or course of action or treatment: as, to go ''through'' an operation; to go ''through'' college (that is, a course of instruction in college); to go ''through'' a course of treatment or training.}}

'''2.''' Among: expressing a succession of experiences in passing along any course to ultimate exit or emergence: as, to pass ''through'' perils or tribulations.

{{smaller block|And I must blame all you that may advise him;<br>That, having help'd him ''through'' all martial dangers,<br>You let him stick at the kind rites of peace.
{{right|''Chapman'', Byron's Conspiracy, iv. 1.}}}}

'''3.''' By way of: expressing a preliminary or intermediate stage.

{{smaller block|The brown plain far and wide<br>Changed year by year ''through'' green to hoary gold.
{{right|''William Morris'', Earthly Paradise, II. 201.}}}}

'''4.''' By means of: expressing instrumentality, means, or agency.

{{smaller block|It is ''through'' me they have got this corner of the Court to cozen in.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Mercury Vindicated.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
{{smaller block|All salvation is ''through'' Christ.
{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Religio Medici, i. 54.}}}}

¶
5. By reason of; on account of; in consequence of; out of: expressing reason or actuating principle or impulse: as, to run away ''through'' fear.

{{smaller block|He rested him on the floore, unfitte ''through'' his rusticity for a better place.
{{right|''Spenser'', To Sir Walter Raleigh.}}}}

{{smaller block|This proceedes ''through'' the barbarous ignoraunce of the time, and pride of many Gentlemen.
{{right|''Puttenham'', Arte of Eng. Poesie, p. 14.}}}}

{{smaller block|I feel my fault, which only was committed
''Through'' my dear love to you.
{{right|''Fletcher'', Humorous Lieutenant, i. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|Cannot you surmise the weakness which I hitherto, ''through'' shame, have concealed even from you?
{{right|''Sheridan'', School for Scandal, i. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|Himself secure in the wise liberality of the successive administrations ''through'' which he had held office, he had been the safety of his subordinates in many an hour of danger and heartquake.
{{right|''Hawthorne'', Scarlet Letter, Int., p. 12.}}}}

'''To break, get, go, look,''' etc., '''through.''' See the verbs.

'''II.''' ''adv.'' 1. From one end or side to the other: as, to pierce or bore a thing ''through.'' See ''thorough, adv.''

{{smaller block|Truth has rough flavours if we bite it ''through.''
{{right|''George Eliot'', Armgart, ii.}}}}

2. From beginning to end: as, to read a letter ''through.''—3. To the end; to the ultimate purpose: as, to carry a project ''through.''—4. To the end or terminal point, as of a line of

readings.c3s2:
travel: as, that ticket will take you ''through.''—5†. Thoroughly.

{{smaller block|I protest
Myself ''through'' rarified, and turned all flame
In your affection. {{right|''B. Jonson,'' Sejanus, ii. 1.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Through and through,''' thoroughly; out and out: as, a method ''through and through'' speculative.—'''To bear, carry, fall, put,''' etc., '''through.''' See the verbs.—'''To be through,''' to have finished; have done: as, ''are'' you ''through?'' [Colloq.]—'''To drop through,''' to fall to pieces; come to naught; fail or perish: same as ''to fall through:'' as, the scheme ''dropped through.''}}

{{smaller block|Through idleness . . . the house ''droppeth through.''
{{right|Eccl. x. 18.}}}}

'''through'''<sup>1</sup> (thrö), ''a.'' [< ''through''<sup>1</sup>, ''adv.'' Cf. ''thorough, a.''] 1. Clear; open; unobstructed.

{{smaller block|Was there not a ''through'' way then made by the swoord for the imposing of lawes uppon them?
{{right|''Spenser,'' State of Ireland.}}}}

2. That extends or goes with little or no interruption or without change from one important or distant place to another: as, a ''through'' line of railway; a ''through'' train; a ''through'' passenger.—3. That entitles to transportation to the end of the line or succession of lines by which some distant point is reached: as, a ''through'' ticket; a ''through'' bill of lading.—{{smaller|'''Through bolt,''' a bolt which passes through from side to side of what it fastens.—'''Through bridge.''' See ''bridge.''—'''Through coal,''' the name given in the South Wales coal-field to a mixture of large and small coal. Also called ''altogether coal,'' and in Somersetshire ''brush-coal.'' None of these terms are used in the United States.—'''Through fang.''' See ''fang.''—'''Through rate,''' a rate or price charged for carrying goods or passengers to a distant destination, over the routes of various}}

